Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou <Janis_Papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote:
> 
>> Though WRT Kent's posted list of "goals" I also
>> agree with the poster who said "That and most of
>> your list aren't really game goals.".
> 
> Since I presented them as "measures of playing
> skill", so what?
> 
> Let's pull that list back into the foreground, and
> consider it in the way I presented it, as
> demonstrations of skill, not in some bogus "game
> goals" way others may want to force onto it.
> 
> :- NetHack is just far too complex a game for any
> :- one dimensional totally orderable score system to
> :- capture the skill with which a game was played,
> :- or the progress which was achieved. How would one
> :- summarize in a single score:
> 
> :- Thoroughness with which levels were explored.

Not demonstrably indicative of skill due to random placement of
downstairs and random map arrangement potentially requiring one to
explore entire level.  Full exploration of Gehennom not skillful,
instead should be considered masochistic.

> :- Management of pets.

Subjective criterion: be more specific, please.

> :- Identification of objects.

Not indicative of skill; cf. illiterate conduct.  It is possible to do
quite well in the game consistently without formally identifying
everything one comes in contact with.

> :- Treatment of peaceful monsters.

Indicative of in-game alignment, not skill.

> :- Abuse of game features.

Subjective criterion: be more specific, please.

> :- Adequacy and locations of stashes.

Using stashes is not necessarily indicative of skill.

> :- Depths reached in all branches.

Any idiot can take a pickaxe and dig down repeatedly.

> :- Accuterment and baggage.

Possibly subjective, but be more specific, please.

> :- Burden management.

Sometimes it is necessary and useful to be burdened.

> :- Evasion of dangerous foes.

Subjective criterion: be more specific on "evasion", please.  Many
monsters are not worth evading.

> :- Luring monsters into traps.

More specific on "luring", please.  How can you tell a "lure" from a
gnome being spawned next to a pit in your view, exactly?

> :- Traps evaded.

Not indicative of skill for three reasons:

1) Random trap location/generation.
2) Most traps are irrelevant after a certain point in the game, so the
player simply steps on them and goes forward.
3) Some traps should be intentionally triggered.

> :- Use of bones piles.

Not sufficiently indicative of skill because bones piles and their
contents are completely random.

> :- Use of traps and other decorations
> :-   (fountains, trees, doors, passages,
> :-   walls) as tools.

Possibly indicative of skill, depending on how corner-case you want to
be with each one; need more specifics.

> :- Wish use or disuse.

It's called "wishless" conduct; currently in the game.

> :- Conducts both universal and personal.

Conducts not listed in the game will, of course, not exist for purposes
of determining in-game score.  Conducts in the game are, of course, in
the game now.

> :- Object employment.

Subjective criterion: more specifics, please.

> :- Skill slot management.

Sometimes it is worthwhile to become Skilled in a thing, even though you
will use it but rarely.

> :- Speed of leveling up.

Not even remotely indicative of skill.  Some classes will not level fast
because they cannot kill things quickly; some classes reach level 7-8
almost by accident.  Some conducts will create artificially low
levelling speeds.

> :- Total of monsters killed, extincted.

Currently the backbone of the ineffective scoring system; perhaps worthy
of an incidental mention in a future scoring system, but not much.

> :- Speed of ascension in turns.

Not as much indicative of play skill as it is of willingness to take
risks.  Which risks are needlessly risky qualifies as a subjective
criterion ...and therefore unuseful.

> :- Speed of play in realtime.

Not as indicative of skill in a turn-based game as people would like to
believe.  It's a turn-based game for a reason.

> :- Alternatives chosen.

Can you be more vague here, maybe?

> :- Alignment adherence.

Role-play criterion, unrelated to skill.

> :- Role adherence.

Role-play criterion, unrelated to skill.

> :- "Style."

Role-play criterion, unrelated to skill.  Oh, and highly subjective.

> Now, respond to that, not to some strawman.

Well, it looks like his statement still stands.  Most of your list isn't
really a good measure of "play skill"... so the task of creating a
single, useful scoring system isn't anywhere near as complex as you're
making it out to be.

